  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1050 PM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nHigh pressure will remain elongated across the area south of the\nFour Corners with another dry day on Monday and limited moisture\nwhich may result in a few isolated showers over the high terrain\nmainly along the Divide and eastern Uintas. With the dry air\nmass in place and dry low levels, wetting rain is not\nanticipated. The ridge will flatten on Tuesday and Wednesday as\na weak frontal boundary and shortwave tracks across the north.\nThis will result in a tightened surface gradient and some\nbreezy afternoon winds with gusts in the 20 to 30 mph range\nmainly across the north. Some isolated to widely scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms will be possible over the high terrain\nboth Tuesday and Wednesday afternoon with gusty outflow winds\nand lightning more of a concern than wetting rain at this point.\nTowards the end of the week, the ridge of high pressure will\nbuild back over the region resulting in a warmup in temperatures\nwith highs rising into the upper 90s and low triple digits for\nthe lower desert valleys of east-central and southeast Utah and\nwest-central Colorado. Residual moisture will work in around the\nhigh, mainly across the south with scattered showers and storms\npossible over the high terrain each afternoon. Otherwise, most\nareas look to remain hot and dry.\n\nLate Friday heading into the weekend, models are indicating a\nlow pressure trough moving onto the Pacific Northwest coast and\npushing further inland. This nudges the high pressure ridge\nfurther east to allow for a more favorable southerly tap of\nmonsoonal moisture to return to the region. As a result,\nstorm/precipitation chances and coverage look to increase with\nthe higher terrain favored each afternoon with some potential\nspread into valleys late in the day. Hot temperatures look to\nremain through Saturday with highs 5 to 10 degrees above normal\nbefore dropping to near normal Sunday into early next week.",
